Falsifies `paging::verification::no_admitted_pair_is_writable_here_and_executable_there`.

The defect: `Flags::makes_writable_executable_pair` asks only one direction, whether the
existing mapping is writable and the new one executable. That is the order the tenth outsider
pass reproduced (`MAP_RW`, then `MAP_CODE`), and it is the natural shortcut for whoever writes
the check against that witness. The reverse order, code first and then a writable mapping, is
then admitted: the mapping hold sees no pair and one page becomes writable at one address and
executable at another.

Expected to fail: `assert!(!(writes(a) && executes(b)) && !(writes(b) && executes(a)))`, for any
`a` executable and `b` writable.

Replayed on 2026-10-31 (UTC) with `cargo kani --harness
no_admitted_pair_is_writable_here_and_executable_there` in `crates/paging`: one failure at that
assertion. The host test `only_writable_beside_executable_makes_the_pair` fails under it too.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/flags.rs b/crates/paging/src/flags.rs
index 66104f9f4..9ca9fcea6 100644
--- a/crates/paging/src/flags.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/flags.rs
@@ -183,7 +183,7 @@ impl Flags {
     pub const fn makes_writable_executable_pair(self, other: Flags) -> bool {
         let self_executable = self.is_user_executable() || self.is_kernel_executable();
         let other_executable = other.is_user_executable() || other.is_kernel_executable();
-        (self.is_writable() && other_executable) || (other.is_writable() && self_executable)
+        self.is_writable() && other_executable
     }
 
